---
import { md } from "@daviddef/archive-kit/lib/md.js";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import corrections from "../data/corrections.json";
import additions from "../data/additions.json";
import Changes from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Changes.astro";

/* A reader arriving at this many corrections needs to be told what moved and when.
   THE DEK IS DERIVED FROM corrections.count, not typed: it said «Sixty-two» until
   11 October 2026 while the stat box three lines below it already rendered 235.
   Newest first. Each entry says what the record was and what it changed. */
const days = [
  {
    date: "11 October 2026, the Sicilian day",
    title: "Simone Nicotra ruled out of two towns, and three false negatives found in this archive's own record",
    found: [
      ["Giarre, Morti 1820\u20131860 \u2014 the D section, all 39 readable years",
       "MARIANNA DAIDONE is in no Giarre death Tavola. The first pass had read M and N together, because they sit side by side, and skipped D in every single year \u2014 and an Italian death index files a married woman under her MAIDEN name. Eight years rule D blank outright; 1841 gives the letter a whole empty page of its own."],
      ["Mascali, Morti 1820\u20131872 \u2014 51 of its 52 years",
       "Neither Simone nor Marianna died at Mascali either. But both surnames LIVE there, which Giarre could never show: SIX Nicotra decedents, and in 1841 the first DAIDONE decedent found anywhere in this project \u2014 Angela Dajdone, aged fifty, \u00abnata in Mascali\u00bb."],
      ["Mascali, Morti 1869 atto 63",
       "ALFIO NICOTRA, died 13 May 1869 aged SEVENTY-THREE, \u00abnato in Giarre\u00bb, \u00abfiglio del fu GIUSEPPE e della fu CATERINA, morta in Giarre\u00bb. He is the father named in both the 1848 and 1851 Mascali Nicotra acts, so GIUSEPPE NICOTRA OF GIARRE stands in Simone's own generation. A brother is the natural reading and is NOT proved."],
      ["Mascali, Morti 1828 atti 49 and 103",
       "Two widows alive at Mascali in 1828 and named by their children's acts: MARIA NICOTRA, widow of Carmelo Tomarchio, and DONNA SANTA DAJDONE, widow of Don Domenico Bassi. Neither ever appears as a decedent in the fifty-one years swept afterwards."],
      ["Giarre, Morti 1842 atto 189",
       "\u00abALFIO NICOTRA D'ANNI SETTANTA, SPOSO DI ROSA PAPPALARDO \u2026 FIGLIO DI FU GIUSEPPE e di FU LEONARDA PENNISI\u00bb. This archive had inferred that identification from the Tavola and never checked it against the act. The act confirms it \u2014 and proves there are TWO Alfio Nicotras, born about 1772 and about 1796, who must not be merged."],
      ["Giarre, Matrimoni 1845\u20131860 \u2014 15 of 16 years",
       "No Nicotra groom in any of them, so Simone had no son who married at Giarre in those years. A marriage act names BOTH sets of parents, which is why this was the right book. 1851 turns out to be ordered by the groom's FORENAME, so a surname search finds nothing while appearing to work."],
      ["Giarre, Morti 1854",
       "The Tavola mapped from its start for the first time. Its D section had never been reached and is now read \u2014 no Daidone. Its N section is NOT unread: the binder put 1856's N-to-Z run where 1854's belongs, so the letters are not in the volume at all."],
    ],
    fixed: [
      "\u00abGIARRE MORTI 1850\u00bb WAS READ IN MACCHIA'S REGISTER. This archive recorded 1850's missing index as recovered from \u00abthe duplicate deposit\u00bb and called it the first time the two-deposit rule had paid. That container is labelled MACCHIA \u2014 a different comune. The year is now marked unread and the sweep stands at 39 of 41 years, not 40.",
      "321 OF THE 808 ROWS IN THE GIARRE ARK LIST WERE MACCHIA REGISTERS, filed as Giarre and counted as Giarre's own second copies. They are split into their own file, and both build tools now know Macchia exists. The tell had been visible all along: those volumes' PATRIA column names Milo, Macchia and Dagala, which are Macchia's hamlets.",
      "\u00abGIARRE HOLDS TWO COPIES OF EVERY YEAR\u00bb was never true, and this page's sibling said so to readers for weeks. The second copy was always the neighbouring town.",
      "NINE DOCUMENTED PEOPLE HAD NO PAGES. They were added to the data, every gate passed and the deploy went green \u2014 but `npm run build` does not rebuild people.json, so none of them reached the site. The archive went from 698 people to 707 once the root chain was actually run.",
      "TWO HEADLINE NUMBERS WERE STALE. This page's own opening line said \u00abSixty-two corrections\u00bb while the stat box three lines below it rendered 235, and the home page offered \u00abSixty-two people across ten generations\u00bb where the ancestors data says 93. Both are now derived from the data instead of typed.",
      "TWENTY SWEEP ROWS still ended \u00abTHE D SECTION, FOR DAIDONE, WAS NOT READ IN THIS PASS\u00bb after it had been read, and the 1842 row dated Alfio Nicotra's death to February when the act says 16 December.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "13\u201314 September 2026, the second run",
    title: "Rocco Arena's own marriage act, and two register series nobody knew were there",
    found: [
      ["Scilla, Matrimoni 1888 atto 63",
       "Rocco Arena married Generosa Costa in DECEMBER 1888 — and his own act names his parents: «figlio di FRANCESCO ANTONIO e di SERAFINA ARLOTTA». The correction this site argued for on five circumstantial supports is now a document."],
      ["The same act",
       "Generosa Costa's parents for the first time: ANTONIO COSTA and ANNA ARLOTTA. Her mother is an Arlotta, like Rocco's own mother — the two families are tied twice over, and Rocco and Generosa may have been cousins."],
      ["Scilla, Matrimoni 1885 atti 73 and 86",
       "Two of Rocco's brothers married seven weeks apart: Giuseppe to Maria Martello on 12 September, Giovanni to Concetta Russo on 29 October. Act 73 writes the father out in full, which proves «Antonio Arena» is Francesco Antonio."],
      ["Scilla, Morti 1891 atto 134",
       "ARENA FORTUNATO, ten days old, died at Via Grotte 70 on 1 October 1891 — a son of Giovanni and Concetta Russo, born about 21 September, and not in the family tree."],
      ["Piedimonte Etneo, Matrimoni 1836 atto 17",
       "Mariano Mazza, 30, bracciale, son of IGNAZIO and GIUSEPPA PULVIRENTI, married Agata Catalano of Macchia. Recorded as a LEAD, not a fact — the bride's given name and the groom's age both sit awkwardly."],
    ],
    fixed: [
      "This site said Antenati's Scilla marriages ran 1905–1913. They run 1866–1913 — forty-eight volumes — alongside 46 of banns and 81 of deaths. The error had blocked at least three searches.",
      "The 1836 index was read here as «Mazza ORAZIO»; the act says IGNAZIO, and the argument built on the misreading — that Rosario named a son Orazio — is withdrawn.",
      "Two leads from the death indexes were opened and one failed: Arena Raffaela «fu Antonio» is the daughter of Antonio Arena and TERESA PONTILLO, not of Francesco Antonio. Francesco Antonio's death is still unfound.",
      "The Orazio Arena who died at Scilla in 1895 is NOT the Orazio the family tree gives Rocco as a father: his wife is Concetta Maria Nestro and his parents Antonio Arena and Serafina Deluca. A third household.",
      "For about an hour The Crossings said Clementina Arena's 1921 crossing was not found. FindMyPast already had it. Two free indexes of the same manifests disagree about who is in them.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "13 September 2026",
    title: "Two register series opened, and four generations came out of them",
    found: [
      ["Piedimonte Etneo, Matrimoni 1871 atto 14",
       "Rosario Mazza and Leonarda Bonanno married 30 April 1871 — both single, both born there, both braccianti. All four parents named, hers for the first time anywhere: Gaetano Bonanno and Anna Cavallaro."],
      ["Piedimonte Etneo, Nati 1845 atto 64",
       "Rosario born 2 June 1845 at eleven in the morning, declared by his father Mariano, 36, a bracciale, with Maria Catalano, 36. The tree says 1850."],
      ["Piedimonte Etneo, Nati 1843 atto 101",
       "Leonarda Bonanno born 7 November 1843. Gaetano Bonanno dated to about 1807, Anna Cavallaro to about 1805."],
      ["Scilla, Nati 1854 atto 76",
       "Giovanni Arena born 30 April 1854, declared by his grandfather GIOVANNI ARLOTTA, 75, marinaro, «figlio del fu DOMENICO» — the deepest name in this archive."],
      ["Scilla, Matrimoni 1885 atti 73 and 86",
       "Giuseppe Arena married Maria Martello on 12 September; Giovanni Arena married Concetta Russo on 29 October. Two of Rocco's brothers, seven weeks apart, both sons of Francesco Antonio Arena and Serafina Arlotta."],
    ],
    fixed: [
      "The 1854 index gave the mother as ROSA Arlotta and the date as 20 April; the act says SERAFINA and the thirtieth. Giovanni Arena is Rocco's BROTHER, not his cousin.",
      "Three Giovanni Arena this site kept apart are ONE MAN — born 1854, married 1885, father of Giuseppe in 1892, sailing in 1899. The birth search had failed because it trusted a declared age three years out.",
      "The 1836 Piedimonte index was read here as «Mazza ORAZIO»; the act says IGNAZIO. The argument built on that reading — that Rosario named a son Orazio — is withdrawn.",
      "This site said Scilla's marriages on Antenati ran 1905–1913. They run 1866–1913, forty-eight volumes, and the error had blocked at least three searches.",
      "For about an hour this page said Clementina Arena's 1921 crossing was not found. It was found months ago, on FindMyPast. Two free indexes of the same manifests disagree about who is in them.",
    ],
  },
];

/* HARMONISED 11 October 2026. The estate check counts a page that three or more
   archives carry as a shared idea, and seven of the eight already drew this one
   with the kit's Changes component while this archive drew it by hand.
   THE DATA ABOVE IS UNTOUCHED — only the rendering changed. The two-part shape
   this page has always had, «what the records gave» against «what this archive
   had wrong», survives as the component's `kind`, which it renders as a chip and
   turns into filter buttons. A retraction's first sentence becomes its title and
   the rest its body, because these were written as prose and have no short name. */
const firstSentence = (t) => {
  const i = t.indexOf(". ");
  return i > 0 && i < 140 ? [t.slice(0, i + 1), t.slice(i + 2)] : [t, ""];
};
const kitDays = days.map((d) => ({
  when: d.date,
  id: d.date.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-").replace(/^-|-$/g, ""),
  entries: [
    ...(d.found || []).map(([src, what]) => ({ title: md(src), found: md(what), kind: "from a register" })),
    ...(d.fixed || []).map((f) => {
      const [head, rest] = firstSentence(f);
      return { title: md(head), did: rest ? md(rest) : null, kind: "taken back" };
    }),
  ],
}));
---
<Base title="What changed" eyebrow="A log of the days this archive moved"
      dek={`${corrections.count} corrections is too many to arrive at cold. This is what moved, when, and what the record was — including the things this archive got wrong and had to take back.`}>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{corrections.count}</div><div class="l">corrections and additions</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{corrections.documented}</div><div class="l">resting on a record that was read</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{additions.filter((a) => a.inTree === "\u2014").length}</div><div class="l">people absent from the family tree</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="note" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem"><strong>What the records gave</strong> and
    <strong>what this archive had wrong</strong> are different things, and the chips below keep them
    apart — reading a register for six hours and finding nothing is a day's work and no discovery.
    Every retraction is still on the page where it was first argued, with the reasoning that led to
    it. Nothing has been quietly deleted.</p>
    <Changes days={kitDays} u={u} />
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="note"><strong>This page is curated, and it has a hole in it.</strong> It records the
    days somebody sat down and wrote them up, not every day the archive moved — and between
    <strong>14 September and 11 October 2026</strong> more than three hundred commits landed that are
    not described here. The honest record of those is the commit log and
    <a href={u("/searched/")}>What Has Been Read</a>, which is generated from the data and cannot
    fall behind it. If the newest date above is far behind today, trust those two and not this.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Where to read the detail</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><a href={u("/corrections")}>For Michael</a> is the list itself, grouped by kind, with the
      source for every line. <a href={u("/scilla")}>Scilla</a> and <a href={u("/mazza")}>The
      Mazza</a> carry the acts and the arguments. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open Questions</a>
      says what is still not known and what would settle it, and
      <a href={u("/searched")}>What Has Been Read</a> says which volumes have been opened — including
      the ones that came back empty.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
